PLACES=[
N('connecticut-street-armory-buffalo','Connecticut Street Armory (74th Regiment Armory)','Buffalo','wny','fort','standing',42.90389,-78.89417,'site','view','drive','','1899',
  'A National Guard armory built in 1899 for the 74th Regiment, to designs by Isaac G. Perry, on ground that had held a city reservoir. It is a castle of Medina sandstone: an administration block ringed by towers of four to six stories, with a square tower over the main door and a two-story drill shed behind, all on a battered stone base. It was listed on the National Register in 1995.',
  'The sandstone towers and battlements, best seen from Niagara Street and from Columbus Park beside it. Interior not checked.',
  'A castle built for drill practice.',
  'A National Guard armory. Look from Connecticut Street, Niagara Street or Columbus Park; whether the public may go in was not checked.',
  [W('Connecticut Street Armory'),NRB],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('blessed-trinity-church-buffalo','Blessed Trinity Church','Buffalo','wny','worship','standing',42.9325,-78.83417,'site','open','drive','','1923–1928',
  'A Roman Catholic parish church in the Highland Park neighbourhood, built from 1923 in the Lombard Romanesque manner of 12th-century northern Italy; the Register list gives 1923 to 1928. It is built of hand-made, medieval-style brick and is said to carry more coloured architectural terra cotta than any other church in the country. The complex also has a church school of 1907 and a parish house of 1914, and was listed on the National Register in 1979.',
  'The brick front with its carved portals and coloured terra cotta; the Register list notes mosaics inside. The church holds Masses that anyone may attend.',
  'A copy of a 12th-century Italian church, in Buffalo brick.',
  'An active parish of the Diocese of Buffalo; the Mass times are from the parish\'s own page. Street parking on the surrounding streets (not checked).',
  [W('Blessed Trinity Roman Catholic Church Buildings'),L('Blessed Trinity parish','https://blessedtrinitybuffalo.org/home/')],brick='yes',
  hours='Masses Saturday 4:30 p.m., Sunday 10 a.m.; weekdays Monday to Wednesday and Friday 11 a.m.'),
N('electric-tower-buffalo','Electric Tower','Buffalo','wny','civic','standing',42.8886,-78.8719,'exact','view','drive','','1912',
  'A 14-story Beaux-Arts office tower of 1912 by James A. Johnson, 294 feet tall, at Washington and Genesee streets. It copies the temporary Electric Tower of the 1901 Pan-American Exposition, which was torn down after the fair. Built as the Niagara Mohawk Building and later added to in 1923 and 1928, it is clad in white terra cotta and its octagonal tower steps back three times to a lantern. It was Buffalo\'s tallest building from 1912 to 1925 and was listed on the National Register as the General Electric Tower.',
  'The white terra-cotta tower and lantern, lit in changing colours at night. It hosts Buffalo\'s New Year\'s Eve ball drop. Interior not checked.',
  'The 1901 fair\'s Electric Tower, built again to last.',
  'A private office building. Look from Washington, Genesee or Main Street.',
  [W('Electric Tower'),NRB],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('county-and-city-hall-buffalo','Old Erie County Hall (County and City Hall)','Buffalo','wny','civic','standing',42.884167,-78.878333,'exact','view','drive','','1871–1875',
  'A granite city hall and courthouse built from 1871 to 1875 to designs by Andrew Jackson Warner of Rochester, its cornerstone laid on June 24, 1872. Its clock tower rises 270 feet. It held both the city\'s and the county\'s offices until the city moved to the new City Hall from 1929; it now holds Erie County court offices and records. Listed on the National Register in 1976, within the Joseph Ellicott Historic District.',
  'The granite front and the clock tower over Franklin Street. Interior not checked.',
  'Buffalo\'s first city hall, still a courthouse.',
  'County court offices. Look from Franklin and Church streets; going in on court business was not checked.',
  [W('County and City Hall'),NRB],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('old-post-office-buffalo','Old Post Office (SUNY Erie City Campus)','Buffalo','wny','civic','standing',42.88175,-78.87256,'exact','view','drive','','1897–1901',
  'Buffalo\'s main post office from 1901 to 1963, at 121 Ellicott Street. The Gothic Revival building was begun in 1897 under the federal Supervising Architect and cost $1.5 million; a tower of 244 feet stands over the main door, with deep arches, gargoyles and a green tile roof. Its interior is laid round a roofed courtyard. It was listed on the National Register in 1972 and has been the city campus of SUNY Erie, the county\'s community college, since 1981.',
  'The tower, the gargoyles and the green tile roof. The roofed courtyard inside is a college building; visiting it was not checked.',
  'A Gothic post office that became a college.',
  'A college campus. Look from Ellicott, Swan and Oak streets.',
  [W('Old Post Office (Buffalo, New York)'),NRB],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('buffalo-city-hall','Buffalo City Hall','Buffalo','wny','civic','standing',42.88659,-78.8793,'exact','open','drive','','1929–1931',
  'The city\'s 32-story Art Deco seat of government on Niagara Square, 398 feet tall, designed by Dietel, Wade & Jones and finished in 1931. It is faced with Ohio sandstone and gray limestone over a granite base, with friezes by Albert Stewart, sculpture by Rene Paul Chambellan and bands of polychrome terra cotta near the top. It was listed on the National Register in 1999.',
  'The Art Deco lobby and halls, and an observation deck on the 28th floor about 360 feet up, with views over Lake Erie; elevators go to the 25th floor and stairs the rest of the way.',
  'An Art Deco city hall with a deck at the top.',
  'City offices. Explore Buffalo, a local nonprofit, gives free guided tours at noon; the tour page read gives no days, and the encyclopedia article says weekdays. Parking in downtown lots and on the street.',
  [W('Buffalo City Hall'),L('Explore Buffalo: City Hall tours','https://explorebuffalo.org/downtown/buffalo-city-hall/')],brick='no',
  hours='Free guided tour at noon (weekdays, per the encyclopedia; the tour page gives no days)'),
N('ellicott-square-building-buffalo','Ellicott Square Building','Buffalo','wny','civic','standing',42.8822,-78.875,'exact','view','drive','','1896',
  'An office block of ten stories designed by Charles Atwood of D. H. Burnham & Company and finished in May 1896, built in less than a year for $3.5 million. It was the largest office building in the world by floor area until 1908. In 1896 and 1897 it housed the Vitascope Theater, the earliest known room built only for showing motion pictures. It is named after Joseph Ellicott, who laid out Buffalo.',
  'The Italian Renaissance front of granite, iron, terra cotta and pale gray brick, and an inner court with a marble mosaic floor. Whether the court is open to passers-by was not checked.',
  'The world\'s biggest office building, in 1896.',
  'A private office building. Look from Main, Swan and Washington streets.',
  [W('Ellicott Square Building')],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('guaranty-building-buffalo','Guaranty Building (Prudential Building)','Buffalo','wny','civic','standing',42.88278,-78.87667,'exact','open','drive','','1894–1896',
  'An early skyscraper by Adler & Sullivan, built in 1895 and 1896 and opened in April 1896: the last work of Dankmar Adler and Louis Sullivan together. It was renamed the Prudential Building after a loan from that company, damaged by fire in 1974 and threatened with demolition, then restored from 1981 to 1983 and given its first name back. It is a National Historic Landmark. A law firm that owns it opened a visitor centre on the ground floor in 2017.',
  'Every surface outside is covered in Sullivan\'s cast ornament. The ground-floor visitor centre has the building\'s story; its hours were not found.',
  'Louis Sullivan\'s last skyscraper with Adler.',
  'Offices, with a visitor centre on the ground floor (opened 2017). Hours not checked: the pages that give them could not be opened when this was written. On Church and Pearl streets.',
  [W('Guaranty Building (Buffalo, New York)'),NRB],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('hotel-lafayette-buffalo','Hotel Lafayette','Buffalo','wny','hotel','standing',42.884444,-78.872778,'exact','open','drive','','1902–1926',
  'A seven-story French Renaissance hotel on Lafayette Square, built in stages from 1902 to 1926. It is counted the masterpiece of Louise Blanchard Bethune, the first professional woman architect in the United States; later wings were by Esenwein & Johnson, and the lobby was redone in Art Moderne style in 1942. After decades of decline it was restored for $35 million, finished in 2012, and now holds a hotel, apartments, bars and restaurants. Listed on the National Register in 2010.',
  'The red brick and white terra-cotta front and the 1942 lobby.',
  'The hotel of America\'s first woman architect.',
  'A working hotel with bars and restaurants, so the lobby can be seen by guests and diners; hours not checked.',
  [W('Hotel Lafayette'),NRB],brick='yes',hours='?'),
N('liberty-building-buffalo','Liberty Building','Buffalo','wny','civic','standing',42.8853,-78.87484,'exact','view','drive','','1925',
  'A 23-story neoclassical office tower at 420 Main Street, built in 1925 as the headquarters of Liberty National Bank, which had been the German American Bank until the First World War. To mark the new name the bank put up copies of the Statue of Liberty by Leo Lentelli: two on the roof facing east and west, and a third over the door, since moved to the Court Street entrance. It was the largest office building downtown when new.',
  'The two Lady Liberty statues on the roof, lit at night, and the third at the Court Street entrance. Interior not checked.',
  'Two Statues of Liberty on a Buffalo roof.',
  'A private office building. Look from Main Street and Lafayette Square.',
  [W('Liberty Building (Buffalo, New York)')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('buffalo-savings-bank-dome','Buffalo Savings Bank (the gold dome)','Buffalo','wny','civic','standing',42.88877,-78.87305,'exact','view','drive','','1901',
  'A neoclassical Beaux-Arts bank at 1 Fountain Plaza, opened in May 1901. Its dome, 23 feet tall and 56 feet across, is covered with 13,500 terra-cotta tiles, first coppered and since gilded three times. The bank failed in 1991; the building is now an M&T Bank branch with a large 1983 addition on its north side, and is a City of Buffalo landmark.',
  'The gold dome and the nine-foot clock over the columned front. Interior not checked.',
  'A bank with a gold-leaf dome.',
  'A working bank branch; look from Main and Huron streets.',
  [W('Buffalo Savings Bank')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('sheas-buffalo-theatre','Shea\'s Buffalo Theatre','Buffalo','wny','civic','standing',42.89176,-78.87315,'site','open','drive','','1926',
  'A movie palace by Rapp and Rapp of Chicago that opened on January 16, 1926, built in a year for just over $1.9 million in a mix of Spanish and French Baroque and Rococo. Louis Comfort Tiffany designed the interior, and most of it is still in place. Volunteers saved its contents and its organ when the owners were about to strip it, and it now stages touring Broadway shows.',
  'The Tiffany interior with its crystal chandeliers, and one of the few theatre organs still working in the house it was built for. The theatre gives a monthly historic tour on Monday evenings, booked ahead.',
  'A 1926 movie palace with its Tiffany interior and organ intact.',
  'Run by Shea\'s Performing Arts Center; times are from its own pages. Tours must be booked a month ahead, online, by phone or at the box office next door at 650 Main Street.',
  [W('Shea\'s Performing Arts Center'),L('Shea\'s: historic tours','https://www.sheas.org/historic-tours/'),L('Shea\'s: plan your visit','https://www.sheas.org/plan-your-visit-buffalo/')],brick='?',
  hours='Box office Monday to Friday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., Saturday 10 a.m. to 2 p.m.; historic tour one Monday a month at 6 p.m.'),
N('st-joseph-cathedral-buffalo','St. Joseph Cathedral','Buffalo','wny','worship','standing',42.8828,-78.8782,'exact','open','drive','','1851–1863',
  'The cathedral of the Catholic Diocese of Buffalo, at 50 Franklin Street. Bishop John Timon laid its cornerstone in 1851; it was dedicated unfinished in 1855, its south tower was done in 1862, and it was consecrated in 1863. A larger cathedral on Delaware Avenue took its place from 1915, and this one became the "Old Cathedral"; when the new one was found too costly to repair it was torn down in 1977, and this church became the cathedral again.',
  'Three lancet windows over the altar made for the Paris Exposition of 1855 and given at Ludwig II of Bavaria\'s direction, and a Hook & Hastings organ of 3,627 pipes built for the 1876 Centennial Exposition in Philadelphia.',
  'A cathedral organ from the 1876 Centennial.',
  'An active cathedral; the Mass times are from its page on the diocese\'s site. Doors open shortly before Mass.',
  [W('St. Joseph Cathedral (Buffalo, New York)'),L('Diocese of Buffalo: St. Joseph Cathedral','https://www.buffalodiocese.org/st-joseph-cathedral/')],brick='?',
  hours='Mass Saturday 4:30 p.m., Sunday 10:30 a.m.; Monday and Wednesday to Friday 12:10 p.m., Tuesday 5:15 p.m.'),
N('st-pauls-cathedral-buffalo','St. Paul\'s Episcopal Cathedral','Buffalo','wny','worship','standing',42.882778,-78.876389,'exact','open','drive','','1849–1851',
  'The cathedral of the Episcopal Diocese of Western New York, on a three-cornered lot between Church, Pearl, Erie and Main streets. Richard Upjohn designed it, and it was built from 1849 to 1851 of red Medina sandstone. Fire gutted the inside in 1888 and Robert W. Gibson redesigned it. It is a National Historic Landmark (1987).',
  'The red sandstone nave with its buttresses and pinnacles, and the tower and main door at the southwest corner. The inside is the 1888 rebuilding; details not checked.',
  'Richard Upjohn\'s red sandstone cathedral.',
  'An active cathedral; service times are from its own visit page, which also has a parking page.',
  [W('St. Paul\'s Cathedral (Buffalo, New York)'),L('St. Paul\'s Cathedral: visit','https://www.spcbuffalo.org/visit'),NRB],brick='no',
  hours='Sunday Holy Eucharist 8 a.m. and 10:15 a.m. (with choir); office Monday to Friday 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('westminster-presbyterian-buffalo','Westminster Presbyterian Church','Buffalo','wny','worship','standing',42.90381,-78.87337,'exact','open','drive','','1858–1859',
  'A Romanesque Revival church at 724 Delaware Avenue, founded in 1854 by forty residents including Jesse Ketchum, on land he gave. The sanctuary, by Harlow W. Wilcox, was begun in 1858 and finished in 1859 for $19,200, of light yellow brick with a 200-foot steeple. Tiffany Studios remade the interior in 1903 in English Tudor Gothic; most of the Tiffany windows were replaced between 1931 and 1952 by Gothic Revival glass by Willet, Connick, Burnham and Reynolds. It stands in the Delaware Avenue Historic District.',
  'The yellow brick front and steeple; inside, thirty-one stained glass windows of 1931 to 1952 and one Tiffany window left from 1903.',
  'A Delaware Avenue church with windows by four of America\'s great glassmakers.',
  'An active congregation; worship times are from its own page. Its page mentions a main parking lot and accessible spaces on the south-side driveway.',
  [W('Westminster Presbyterian Church (Buffalo, New York)'),L('Westminster: frequently asked questions','https://wpcbuffalo.org/about-us/faq')],brick='no',
  hours='Sunday 8:15 a.m. (chapel) and 10:30 a.m. (sanctuary), September to May; 10:30 a.m. in the chapel, mid-June to August'),
N('lafayette-high-school-buffalo','Lafayette High School','Buffalo','wny','civic','standing',42.92084,-78.8847,'exact','view','drive','','1901–1903',
  'A French Renaissance Revival high school at 370 Lafayette Avenue by Esenwein & Johnson, of stone, brick and terra cotta, finished in time for its first graduating class in 1903. It was Buffalo\'s third high school and the oldest still in its first building. Its lantern on the roof decayed and was taken down in the 1970s; the alumni association restored it in 1999. The school has a new name since 2015, but classes are still held in the building. Listed on the National Register in 1980.',
  'The front and the restored roof lantern, from Lafayette Avenue.',
  'A 1903 school that got its cupola back.',
  'A working public school: look from Lafayette Avenue only.',
  [W('Lafayette High School (Buffalo, New York)'),NRB],brick='yes',hours='?'),
N('fosdick-masten-park-high-school','Fosdick-Masten Park High School (City Honors School)','Buffalo','wny','civic','standing',42.90306,-78.86056,'exact','view','drive','','1912–1914',
  'An H-shaped school by Esenwein & Johnson, built from 1912 to 1914 of brick sheathed in white glazed terra cotta, on a 5.2-acre site. It has housed City Honors School, a public college-preparatory school, since 1980. Shafts dug before a rebuilding in 2007 found human remains, because the school stands on the site of an old potter\'s field. Listed on the National Register in 1983.',
  'The white terra-cotta front. Interior not checked.',
  'A white terra-cotta high school on an old potter\'s field.',
  'A working public school: look from the street only.',
  [W('Fosdick-Masten Park High School'),W('City Honors School'),NRB],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('mckinley-monument-buffalo','McKinley Monument','Buffalo','wny','curio','standing',42.88639,-78.87806,'exact','public','drive','','1907',
  'A 96-foot marble obelisk in Niagara Square, the hub of Buffalo\'s street plan, in front of City Hall. New York State put it up for William McKinley, who was shot at the Pan-American Exposition in Buffalo on September 6, 1901; it was dedicated on the same date in 1907. Carrère and Hastings, who had led the fair\'s design, drew it, and Alexander Phimister Proctor carved the animals. It was restored in 2017.',
  'The obelisk of Vermont and Italian marble with sleeping lions and turtles round its base.',
  'The monument to a president shot at the 1901 fair.',
  'In the middle of Niagara Square, a public square; any day.',
  [W('McKinley Monument')],brick='no',hours=''),
N('soldiers-sailors-monument-buffalo','Soldiers and Sailors Monument','Buffalo','wny','curio','standing',42.88568,-78.87382,'exact','public','drive','','1882–1884',
  'Buffalo\'s Civil War monument in Lafayette Square, by sculptor Casper Buberl and architect George Keller; the cornerstone was laid on July 4, 1882 and it was dedicated on July 4, 1884. A ten-foot female figure standing for Buffalo tops the shaft, and four bronze figures of infantry, artillery, cavalry and navy face the four points of the compass.',
  'The bronze soldiers and sailor, the bronze reliefs round the column with Lincoln\'s cabinet, and part of the Gettysburg Address on the east side.',
  'The Civil War column in Lafayette Square.',
  'In Lafayette Square, a public square downtown; any day.',
  [W('Soldiers and Sailors Monument (Buffalo, New York)')],brick='no',hours=''),
]
